==French==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|frm|fr}}, from {{etyl|fro|fr}} {{m|fro|donjon}}, {{m|fro|dongon||castle keep}}, from {{etyl|frk|fr}} {{m|frk||*dungjo, *dunjon-|dungeon, bower, underground cellar}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|fr}} {{m|gem-pro|*dungijǭ}}, {{m|gem-pro|*dungō||enclosed space, vault, bower, treasury}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|fr}} {{m|ine-pro|*dʰengʰ-||to cover}}. Cognate with {{etyl|ang|-}} {{m|ang|dung||prison, dungeon}}, {{etyl|osx|-}} {{m|osx|dung||underground cellar}}, {{etyl|goh|-}} {{m|goh|tung||underground cellar}}, {{etyl|non|-}} {{m|non|dyngja||a lady's bower}}. More at [[dung]].

Alternate etymology traces Old French ''donjon'', from Vulgar Latin ''*dominio'' ‘lord's castle’, from Latin [[dominus]].

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/dɔ̃ʒɔ̃/|lang=fr}}

===Noun===
{{fr-noun|m}}

# [[donjon#English|donjon]], [[keep]]

